--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (5/6 key facts kept) ---
I pawned my husband's silver watch for ten shillings, to pay my rent; and I redeemed it next day for eleven shillings.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (0/3 key facts kept) ---
To-day I spoke with Mr. Walter Thomas about what the newspapers say: the New Year opens gloomily; more than two and a half million people are out of work, and the coal and cotton trades are in a bad way.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (4/4 key facts kept) ---
To-day Mr. Thomas set Mr. Hale to finish his quarterly ledgers, and resolved in future to examine them himself, and not to leave them to the clerks, feeling gloomy about the new year, which opens with more than two and a half million out of work, and the coal and cotton trades in a bad way.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that her sister Lizzie was dead, and that she was following her to the grave.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he was reading Trollope in an easy-chair, and listening to the wireless talks on Sunday evening.

